Both columns compute the SAME tiles and produce the SAME output --
verified per row before timing. The ratio is the traffic term the
element-count model cannot see, and it is the only quantity found
so far that differs by transform at a fixed tile shape.
FIRST NUMBER = coalescing only, at A rows. It is a LOWER BOUND on
the class B penalty, NOT the penalty: class B touches 1.94x-15.5x
more rows depending on tile shape. A small first number does NOT
close the question -- that inference was proposed and is wrong.
The R multipliers sweep the row-count axis so the two factors can
be composed for any class B configuration without implementing it.

